{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "Crime and Punishment"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Crime and Technology"}, {"type": "position", "content": 1}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.3"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Identify and describe rules and obligations through a wider range of language and expressions in both work and social contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. "}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. "}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": "Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. "}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": "Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. "}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. "}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. "}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. "}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. "}, {"type": "secondary_9", "content": "Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. "}, {"type": "secondary_10", "content": "Writes well-organized texts or articles about explaining the rules and regulations using appropriate grammar, vocabulary, spelling coherence and cohesion, and different levels of formality. "}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Past Perfect Tense"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use the Past Perfect tense to identify and talk about an 'earlier past'."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Fingerprints found at the scene of the crime had been the most important clues to a criminal's identity before the introduction of \"genetic fingerprinting\"."}, {"type": "text", "content": "When you are talking about a past event, you can refer to something that happened at an earlier time to that past event by using the Past Perfect tense. <br /><br />  The structure of the Past Perfect is:<br /><br />  Subject + had + past participle<br /><br /><br />   <span class=\"italic\">When I arrived at the party, Vanessa <span class=\"strong\">had </span>already <span class=\"strong\">gone </span>home. <br /></span> Which event happened <span class=\"italic\">first</span>? Vanessa went home.<br /> Which event happened <span class=\"italic\">later</span>? I arrived at the party.<br /><br />  You use the Past Perfect tense (not Simple Past) when you want to talk about how long something had continued <span class=\"strong\">up to a past moment. </span><br /><br />  <span class=\"italic\">Until recently, detectives <span class=\"strong\">had used</span> a limited number of tools to help them solve crimes.</span><br />  Detectives used a limited number of tools from the past until recently."}, {"type": "example", "content": "By using another process called \"neutron-activation analysis,\" a hair from the murder victim's head can show if he or she had been drugged or poisoned."}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "The Simple Present Passive"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use an active structure when you want the \u2018doer' of the action to be the subject.<br/>You use a passive structure when you want the 'receiver' of the action to be the subject. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "This technique is used by police around the world."}, {"type": "example", "content": "People can be identified by a hair or a tiny spot of blood. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Suspects are found by the police quickly scanning large amounts of information."}, {"type": "example", "content": "A criminal's car can also be identified by studying pieces of glass or paint found at the scene of the crime. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Active sentence = People (subject) <span class=\"strong\">consider</span> murder (object) to be the most serious crime.<br /><br />  Passive sentence = Murder (subject) <span class=\"strong\">is considered </span>to be the most serious crime (by people).<br/>It is not necessary to mention the object in a passive sentence. <br /><br /><br />   You make the passive form of a verb by using tenses of the <span class=\"italic\">be</span> verb followed by the past participle. <br /><br />  The Simple Present passive form is:<br /><br />  am/are/is + past participle<br /><br />  For example: <br /><span class=\"italic\">English <span class=\"strong\">is spoken</span> in the United States.</span>"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "clue "}, {"type": "definition", "content": " a piece of evidence or information used in the detection of a crime "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: A Frenchman named Edmond Locard noticed that criminals always left clues at the scene of the crime. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn8le03ob01aud01"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to talk about crime and technology. "}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "limited"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "restricted in amount "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Until recently, detectives had used a limited number of tools to help them solve crimes."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn8le03ob01aud02"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "tool "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a device used to carry out a particular function"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Detectives now have many tools to help them."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn8le03ob01aud03"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "genetic fingerprinting"}, {"type": "definition", "content": " using a person's genetic material to identify them"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Fingerprints had been the most important clues to a criminal's identity before \"genetic fingerprinting\" was introduced."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn8le03ob01aud04"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "molecule"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a group of atoms bonded together"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"t